findings.body:
The audience field sets `defaultOperator: 'is any of'`, but the multiselect operator enum in `filters.tsx` uses the key `is_any_of` (line 800). The label `'is any of'` is only the display text (line 128). `addFilterWithOption` (line 1977) falls back to `'is_any_of'` when no default is set, so the explicit value is not just redundant, it is wrong.

With `hideOperatorSelect: true`, `FilterOperatorDropdown` looks up the label with `operators.find(op => op.value === operator)`. That lookup misses for `'is any of'`, so it falls back to `formatOperator('is any of')`. The stored filter carries an operator string that no multiselect operator matches, so anything that switches on the operator enum will not recognize the audience filter.

::diff{file="apps/stats/src/views/Stats/components/stats-filter.tsx" lines="446-457"}

Delete the line or change it to `'is_any_of'`. The field already gets that default without it.

::callout{type="warning"}
I confirmed the key mismatch from the source. I did not trace the serializer that turns filters into the analytics query, so the exact downstream effect is unverified.

The diff removed `handleClose()` from the non-inline single-select branch and replaced it with `setOpen(false)`. `handleClose` is where this PR added `setSearchInput('')`, so the search query now persists after a single-select pick. Selecting an option in this popover and then reopening it keeps the old query and shows a filtered list.

`setOpen(false)` does not go through the Popover's `onOpenChange`, which is the only other place that clears the search (on a 200 ms timer). So nothing else resets it.

This path is reachable: `FilterValueSelector` renders `SelectOptionsPopover` without `inline` for select and multiselect fields (filters.tsx around lines 1596-1598), so editing an existing chip hits it.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="1270-1281"}

::callout{type="info"}
The removed call was probably meant to be a cleanup for the new multiselect auto-close. The fix is to restore the `handleClose()` call here, or to call `setSearchInput('')` directly.

For multiselect, the inline path calls `onClose?.()` directly (line 1136). The non-inline path calls `handleClose()` (line 1273), which also clears the search. Two paths doing the same 'auto-close' now behave differently, and the inline one can leave a stale query when the parent keeps the component mounted.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="1133-1138"}

Route both branches through one close helper so the search reset and the `onClose` callback stay in sync.

The base code used `const newValues` here. This PR changes it to `var`, which has function scope and is flagged by the `no-var` lint rule that most of this codebase follows. Nothing else in the change needs `var`.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="1124-1126"}

Change it back to `const`.

The changes add `autoCloseOnSelect`, the search reset on close, the `onClose` reset of `selectedFieldKeyForOptions`, and the audience operator default. There are no test files for `filters.tsx` (only `filters.stories.tsx` exists in `apps/shade/src/components/ui/`), and the PR adds no tests. A test that selects a multiselect option with `autoCloseOnSelect` and checks that the popover closes would catch the bad operator key and the dropped search reset above.

::code{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="749-752"}

Add a focused test for the multiselect auto-close and for the audience operator value, using the existing component harness.
